acute above the arch (82 for `á`). The existing native Rust v1 TSV accepts
these strokes but **does not retain exact curve primitives or a distinct
variant label**; replacing the `a` or `á` is an explicit, reversible draft
choice, not automatic shape switching at render time.

The browser regressions select both variants and prove that a failed
accent fit leaves the previous `á` unchanged. Other checks confirm 32
continuously joined upper-arch samples, unchanged lower circle geometry,
explicit stroke order and UTF-8 TSV roundtrip. The default circle radius
can fit the unaccented upper story; a smaller radius (600 µm) is required
for the tested two-storey accent example.

A native integration smoke roundtripped an explicit 163-segment `a` and
`á` library byte-for-byte and exported a one-page PDF and transparent RGBA
PNG with text `a á a`. The exact SVG and native TSV representations remain
intentionally distinguished.

Full baseline glyph coverage, italic/cursive joins, glyph-level variant
persistence and analytic PDF output remain open.

## Exact base-stroke accent composition (partial) — 2026-11-03

The localhost glyph editor can now explicitly construct accented target
strokes from **already authored** Latin base strokes, without a new font or
hidden fallback. The base and its pen order/colors remain unchanged while
an independent target receives one acute, three ordered tilde segments or
two dot-like umlaut strokes.

Supported base/target pairs are vowels with
acute, n with tilde, and u with umlaut, in both cases and either NFC or NFD
exact target form. The editor derives an accent anchor from the source's
visible line endpoints and pen width, then applies the existing bounded
horizontal shift and lift controls. Missing sources, mismatched target
characters, unsupported combining marks and out-of-cell ink refuse before
target replacement or export.

Browser tests cover explicit Unicode identity,
source preservation, accent geometry, and atomically refused edits; privacy
regressions still cover its local-only actions.

The accent only attaches to an explicitly provided stroke base in a one-time
TSV replacement. There is no stored accent-anchor primitive, native curved
PDF path, automatic inventory fill, or general diacritic synthesis. All
remaining P1 glyph-coverage criteria are open.

